Sure! Here's a JavaScript function that takes a sentence as input and returns a new string with the order of adjectives reversed:

```js
function reverseAdjectives(sentence) {
  // Split the sentence into an array of words, splitting on spaces
  const words = sentence.split(' ');

  // Find the index of the last space (which will be just before the period or question mark at the end of the sentence)
  const lastSpaceIndex = sentence.lastIndexOf(' ') - 1;

  // Reverse the order of words after the last space
  const reversedWords = words.slice(lastSpaceIndex + 1).reverse().join(' ');

  // Concatenate the original words before the last space with the reversed words
  return words.slice(0, lastSpaceIndex + 1).concat(reversedWords).join(' ');
}

// Example usage
const sentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park.";
console.log(reverseAdjectives(sentence)); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park."
```

The function first splits the input sentence into an array of words using the `split()` method. It then finds the index of the last space character in the sentence by calling `lastIndexOf()`. This is the position just before the period or question mark at the end of the sentence.

Next, it slices out the portion of the words array that comes after the last space and reverses its order using the `reverse()` method. Finally, it concatenates the original words before the last space with the reversed words using `concat()` and `join()`. The resulting string is returned as the output of the function.

