response:
To improve efficiency for handling large amounts of text, you can consider using memory-mapped files instead of reading the entire text into memory at once. This allows MATLAB to read and process the text directly from disk without loading it into memory, which reduces the memory usage and improves performance. Here's how you can modify the implementation above using memory-mapped files:

```matlab
%Open the text files as memory-mapped files
mmFile1 = mmopen('Text1.txt', 'r');
mmFile2 = mmopen('Text2.txt', 'r');

%Read the contents of the memory-mapped files into cell arrays of words
words1 = mmread(mmFile1, inf, 'char');
words2 = mmread(mmFile2, inf, 'char');

%Convert texts to lowercase and split into words
words1 = lower(strsplit(words1));
words2 = lower(strsplit(words2));

%Close the memory-mapped files
mmclose(mmFile1);
mmclose(mmFile2);

%Find unique words in text1 that do not appear in text2
uniqueWords = setdiff(words1, words2);

%Print the unique words
disp(uniqueWords);
```

This implementation uses the `mmopen()` function to open the text files as memory-mapped files, which allows you to read and write data in them without loading the entire content into memory. The `mmread()` function is used to read the contents of the memory-mapped files into cell arrays of words. Note that we pass `inf` as the second argument to `mmread()` to indicate that we want to read the entire file into memory. Finally, we close the memory-mapped files using the `mmclose()` function.

Using memory-mapped files can significantly reduce the memory usage and improve performance when dealing with very large text files. However, note that memory-mapped files may have higher disk input/output (I/O) overhead than traditional file I/O, so the exact performance gain depends on the specific use case and hardware configuration.

